# Web Frontend

- Follow the project's framework, router, state, and type-checking conventions. Keep routes focused on composition and move queries and domain operations to the appropriate data layer.
- In server-rendered apps, keep secrets and privileged operations on the server. Scope caches and session clients to users where required. Keep interactive client code and serialized data limited to what the view needs.
- For browser sessions, follow provider guidance for cookie flags, refresh, and request forgery protections. For webviews, apply the host's security and bridge rules. Keep untrusted content out of privileged contexts.
- Use semantic HTML, visible keyboard focus, shared design tokens, and locale-aware formatting. Use `dir="auto"` where suitable for user-generated text. Support touch and pointer input where applicable.
- Escape or sanitize untrusted HTML with the established rendering path. Keep long-running work off the main thread when it causes responsiveness problems; maintain protocol compatibility across clients using the same backend.
- Run relevant lint, type, build, and browser tests. Verify behavior in the webview runtime when bundled in a desktop app.
